cards.body:
SAMA defines **compliance risk** broadly: penalties or regulatory action, significant financial loss, or damage to reputation caused by failing to follow regulations, instructions and standards, **including ethical and conduct codes**.

The sources of the rules are wide too:

- laws and SAMA instructions (regulations, rules, principles, frameworks, guides and mandatory circulars);
- rules of other Saudi authorities and of host countries where the bank operates;
- sound banking practice, internal codes of conduct and integrity standards;
- standards from international bodies such as the Basel Committee.

Topics named in the principles include fair market practice, conflicts of interest, treating customers fairly, suitable advice, AML/CTF, proliferation, KYC, fraud, corruption and **handling reports of violations**.

The principles also warn that an action can hurt the bank's reputation even when no rule is broken. Ask: is it lawful, and is it right?

Paragraph 86 asks the compliance programme to include **warning signs** that the bank may be exposed to non-compliance, such as:

- rapid growth of the bank;
- opening new branches;
- high staff turnover;
- changes in programmes;
- bringing automated systems into workflows.

The same principle must **protect whistleblowers** and include incentives in line with SAMA's whistle blowing policy. When you see one of these signs, raise the testing level in that area.

Alongside the bank's own training, SAMA requires **professional certificates** for certain roles. Its circulars currently ask, in outline, that:

- **front-line bank staff** who deal directly with customers (branch staff and their managers, call centres, customer care) hold the **fundamentals of retail banking** certificate;
- staff **directly involved in granting credit to individuals**, and branch managers, hold the **credit adviser** certificate;
- staff of bank remittance centres hold the relevant **banking and money transfer** certificate;
- staff **contracted through third parties** are included.

Certificates must be obtained **within a set period after joining**, and SAMA expects a **minimum share** of the target staff to hold them at all times. The bank's training plan should track who needs which certificate and when.

The exact roles, periods and percentages are set by SAMA's current circulars, which are updated from time to time. Always check the latest circular in the SAMA Rulebook. The consumer protection quest covers how certificates fit front-line conduct duties.

Before the exam, make sure you can explain:

- **who** owns compliance (board, senior management, compliance unit, every employee);
- the CCO's **independence**: reporting line, rights, pay, SAMA non-objection;
- the **yearly** duties: risk assessment, board evaluation, annual report by end of April;
- **paragraph 72**: annual programme, training before starting, reminders for public-facing staff;
- **board rules**: size, independence, induction, committees, notifying penalties;
- **fit and proper** criteria and acting only after SAMA's non-objection;
- the **whistleblowing** policy: unit, channels, confidentiality, protection, stages.
